[quote][b][color=#ff8c00]⚠ For DBVO 1.x only — do not install this on DBVO 2.[/color][/b]

This mod patches the [font="Courier New"]dialoguemenu.swf[/font] + Papyrus stack that Dragonborn Voice Over shipped through 1.1.1 (now under DBVO's [b]OLD FILES[/b] tab). DBVO 2 is a native rewrite with no [font="Courier New"]dialoguemenu.swf[/font] and no Papyrus, so the patched menu here never gets the call that arms the reply and [b]clicking a topic softlocks the conversation[/b] — the topic list collapses and the NPC never answers.

That is tested, not guessed: with DBVO 2 alone the NPC replies in about five seconds, and with this mod installed the very same topic was still stuck half a minute later. Tab does get you out of the menu, so you lose the conversation and not your save. DBVO 2 itself notices too, and writes a "Conflict" warning to its own log naming the patched [font="Courier New"]dialoguemenu.swf[/font].

DBVO 2 also does nearly everything this mod does, natively: the reply is timed off your line's real audio length, [b]npc_response_delay[/b] is the gap, [b]dialogue_volume[/b] is its volume setting, and 2.0.1.6 added manual skip. [b]If you are on DBVO 2, you do not need this mod[/b] — use DBVO 2's own settings.[/quote]

The NPC's reply lands when your voiced line actually ends. A small SKSE plugin watches your line's audio and fires the reply the moment it truly stops. You can also skip your own line, set the volume of your own voice, and tune the gap before the reply.

[i]Pacing and control tweaks for [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/84329]Dragonborn Voice Over (DBVO)[/url] [b]1.x[/b].[/i]

[line]

[heading]Features[/heading]

[list]
[*][b]Reply on line-end[/b]: the NPC answers right after your line truly finishes, plus a small configurable gap, on every line, whatever the voice pack's speed.
[*][b]Manual skip[/b]: press E / left-click to skip your own voiced line and move on, vanilla-dialogue style.
[*][b]Clean cut on skip and interrupt[/b]: skipping fades your in-flight line out without a click; picking a new topic while an NPC is mid-reply cuts that reply too.
[*][b]Player-voice volume[/b]: just your own DBVO line, 0–300%, without touching other audio. Below 100 attenuates; above 100 amplifies (since 1.1.0) — for a pack mastered far quieter than the NPCs. Gain above unity can clip a pack that is already loud, so raise it only as far as it needs.
[*][b]Configurable gap[/b]: the pause after your line ends before the NPC answers, 0–1000 ms (0 = instant).
[*][b]Native SkyUI MCM[/b]: one screen, no MCM Helper dependency.
[*][b]Menu styles for UI overhauls[/b] (1.1.1): keep your interface. The installer can put these tweaks into Untarnished UI's, Dear Diary Dark Mode's (white or warm) or NORDIC UI's own dialogue menu instead of DBVO's stock one.
[/list]

[heading]Requirements[/heading]

[list]
[*]Skyrim Special Edition or Anniversary Edition + SKSE, matched to your game build (SKSE 2.3.1 for Skyrim 1.7.104). [b]On Skyrim 1.7.99 / 1.7.104 you need v1.0.1 or newer of this mod[/b] — 1.0.0's plugin cannot read the format-5 Address Library those builds use and simply won't load.
[*][b]DBVO 1.x[/b] — [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/84329?tab=files&file_id=416153]Dragonborn Voice Over 1.1.1[/url] from the mod page's OLD FILES tab ([b]not[/b] the main DBVO 2 download), plus what DBVO 1.x itself needs: [b]ConsoleUtilSSE NG[/b] (it speaks your line) and [b]JContainers SE[/b] (it reads DBVO's voice-pack settings), both current for your game build. On Skyrim 1.7.104 that means ConsoleUtilSSE NG [b]1.6.1[/b]+ and JContainers SE [b]4.3.2[/b]+ — older builds are refused by SKSE outright, and with these two DBVO 1.x is verified working there. See Compatibility.
[*]SkyUI (for the MCM)
[*]Address Library for SKSE Plugins — v13, "All in One (1.7.104.0)", on the new game builds
[/list]

[heading]Compatibility[/heading]

[list]
[*][b]On Skyrim 1.7.104, update DBVO's two dependencies or nothing will speak — and that will look like this mod's fault.[/b] SKSE 2.3.1 refuses the old builds of both SKSE plugins DBVO 1.x needs, before they even load, and says so in its own pop-up: [font="Courier New"]ConsoleUtilSSE.dll: must be recompiled for new address library[/font] and [font="Courier New"]JContainers64.dll: disabled, incompatible with current version of the game[/font]. ConsoleUtil is what speaks your line and JContainers is what reads DBVO's settings, so with those refused DBVO produces no player voice at all — and this mod, whose whole job is to time the reply to that voice, has nothing to time.

[b]The fix is two downloads, and I have now tested it:[/b] with [b]ConsoleUtilSSE NG 1.6.1[/b] and [b]JContainers SE 4.3.2[/b], DBVO 1.1.1 works on Skyrim 1.7.104. Verified in-engine on 2026-09-09, SKSE 2.3.1: both plugins load, and clicking a topic ran the whole real chain — DBVO read the voice pack through JContainers, spoke the line through ConsoleUtil, armed this mod's menu, and the NPC replied at the end of the audio plus the gap I had set. Two things to watch while you do it: install JContainers' whole [font="Courier New"]SKSE/Plugins/JCData[/font] folder (without it the game dies on startup and the SKSE log still claims the plugin loaded fine), and if SkyUI greets you with "SKYUI ERROR CODE 4 — your Papyrus INI settings are invalid", that is your own [font="Courier New"]Skyrim.ini[/font] Papyrus memory tweak, unrelated to this mod — but dismiss the box before trying to talk to anyone, because it eats the activation key.
[*][b]Also not compatible with Dragonborn ReVoiced (DBReV).[/b] [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/184221]DBReV[/url] is an independent successor that does play DBVO 1.0 voice packs, and it says plainly that patches targeting the legacy DBVO mod must be removed — this mod is one of those. If you are moving to DBReV or to DBVO 2, uninstall this mod first; both of them time the reply natively and neither loads a patched dialoguemenu.swf.
[*][b]SE and AE, one DLL for both.[/b] Built on CommonLibSSE-NG and reaches the engine through the Address Library (addresses resolved at runtime), so the same file runs on every SE and AE build, Steam or GOG, as long as Address Library is installed. Tested on AE/Steam; SE (1.5.97) and GOG run the same build and should work, but are untested. Please report if you hit anything.
[*][b]Skyrim 1.7.104, yes, from v1.0.1 — and the reply timing is verified there.[/b] Tested in-engine on 1.7.104 with SKSE 2.3.1 and Address Library v13: the plugin loads, installs its hooks, and still does the thing it exists for. With a player line playing, it detected the line's real end, cancelled the menu's word-count guess, and fired the NPC's reply after the configured gap; with the same setup and only this mod's DLL removed, the reply landed on the word-count guess instead. The 1.1.0 volume boost is verified the same way: at 250% the plugin's log shows your line's XAudio2 gain read back at 2.5x the engine's value, at 100% no such line is written, and with the plugin removed the check fails. Version 1.0.0 does not load there at all — Skyrim 1.7.99 changed the Address Library database format, and 1.0.0 predates it, so it stops with "Unsupported address library format: 5". If you updated Skyrim and your dialogue timing went back to feeling wrong, that's why: grab 1.0.1.
[*][b]VR, no.[/b] Skyrim VR uses a different dialogue UI and would need a separate build; it isn't provided.
[*][b]DBVO 2, no.[/b] It replaced the whole swf + Papyrus stack with a single DLL; this mod patches that stack and softlocks dialogue on top of it. See the warning at the top.
[*][b]Using a UI overhaul that moves the dialogue menu? Pick your menu in the installer.[/b] This mod ships the whole [font="Courier New"]dialoguemenu.swf[/font], so on an overhaul that has its own DBVO-patched menu one of the two has to win — installing this one used to drag your topic list back to DBVO's default position. From 1.1.1 the installer asks which menu to install: [b]Stock DBVO[/b] (the default), [b]Untarnished UI[/b], [b]Dear Diary Dark Mode[/b] (white or warm) or [b]NORDIC UI[/b]. Those four are that overhaul's own DBVO-patched menu with this mod's changes built into it, so you keep your layout and get the timing, skip and cuts. Pick the matching one and let it overwrite both DBVO's and the overhaul's copy. On an overhaul that isn't in the list, let the overhaul's menu win instead: the [b]volume slider still works[/b] (it lives in the plugin, not the menu), you just lose the skip key and the reply timing — and tell me which overhaul, it is a small job to add.
[*]It overwrites only DBVO's [font="Courier New"]Interface/dialoguemenu.swf[/font] (let it win that conflict, install it after DBVO) and never touches DBVO's own scripts. The [font="Courier New"].esp[/font] is ESL-flagged and takes no load-order slot.
[/list]

[heading]Files and Skyrim builds[/heading]

One DLL for SE and AE (CommonLibSSE-NG + Address Library, addresses resolved at runtime), Steam or GOG. What differs between the uploads is only which Address Library database format the plugin can read:
[list]
[*][b]1.0.0[/b] — Skyrim SE 1.5.97 through AE 1.6.1170 (Address Library formats 1 and 2). Does NOT load on 1.7.99 / 1.7.104. Tested on AE/Steam 1.6.1170; SE 1.5.97 and GOG are the same build but untested.
[*][b]1.0.1[/b] — those builds plus Skyrim 1.7.99 / 1.7.104 (format 5, Address Library v13). No feature change. Tested on 1.7.104 with SKSE 2.3.1.
[*][b]1.1.0[/b] — same builds as 1.0.1; adds the volume boost (slider to 300%). Tested on 1.7.104 with SKSE 2.3.1.
[*][b]1.1.1[/b] — the same files as 1.1.0 (same plugin, scripts and esp, byte for byte); adds the installer's menu-style choice for UI overhauls. Each style tested on 1.7.104 with SKSE 2.3.1.
[/list]
Match SKSE to your game (2.3.1 for 1.7.104) and use the Address Library for your build. Note the distinction on 1.7.104: [b]this mod[/b] runs and is verified there; [b]DBVO 1.x itself[/b] needs ConsoleUtilSSE NG 1.6.1+ and JContainers SE 4.3.2+ to run there — with those two, the whole chain is verified as well (see Compatibility).

[heading]Changelog[/heading]

[b]1.1.1[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Menu styles for UI overhauls.[/b] The installer now asks which dialogue menu to install: stock DBVO, Untarnished UI, Dear Diary Dark Mode (white or warm), or NORDIC UI. Each of the four is that overhaul's own DBVO-patched menu with this mod's changes built in, so your dialogue keeps its layout instead of jumping back to DBVO's default — the "my dialogue options moved" report, fixed.
[*]No change to the plugin, the scripts or the [font="Courier New"].esp[/font]: they are shared by every menu style. If you don't use one of those UI mods, keep "Stock DBVO menu" and nothing about the mod changes.
[*]Each of the four was verified in-engine on Skyrim 1.7.104 the same way the stock menu is: the reply must still be waiting once DBVO's word-count guess would have fired, then land at the line's real end plus the configured gap.
[*][b]Not a change to this mod, but worth knowing:[/b] DBVO 1.1.1 itself now works on Skyrim 1.7.104, with ConsoleUtilSSE NG 1.6.1 and JContainers SE 4.3.2. I tested the whole chain end to end there — see Compatibility. Nothing in this mod had to change for it.
[/list]

[b]1.1.0[/b]
[list]
[*][b]The volume slider now goes to 300%.[/b] Above 100 the plugin amplifies your own DBVO line — the engine's own volume path is clamped at 100%, so this is a second small hook on the engine's volume-apply function that multiplies the XAudio2 voice's gain for your line only. Asked for by a user with a quiet vampire pack. A loud pack will clip above 100, so raise it only as far as it needs.
[*]The MCM shows an info line for each option.
[*]Verified in-engine on 1.7.104: at 250% the plugin's log shows your line's gain read back at 2.5x the engine's value; at 100% nothing changes; with the plugin removed, nothing happens.
[/list]

[b]1.0.1[/b]
[list]
[*]Rebuilt for Skyrim [b]1.7.99 / 1.7.104[/b]. Skyrim's 1.7.99 update changed the Address Library database to format 5, which the 1.0.0 plugin can't read — it refused to load. Verified in-engine on 1.7.104 with SKSE 2.3.1.
[*]The installer now warns, before it overwrites anything, that this mod is for [b]DBVO 1.x only[/b] and softlocks dialogue on DBVO 2.
[*]No gameplay or feature changes. If you're on Skyrim 1.6.1170 or older and 1.0.0 works for you, you don't need this.
[/list]

[heading]Configuration[/heading]

[list]
[*][b]Gap after your line ends (0–1000 ms)[/b]: pause between your line ending and the NPC's reply. 0 = instant.
[*][b]Player voice volume (0–300%)[/b]: volume of your own DBVO voice line only. 100 = unchanged; above 100 amplifies (can clip a loud pack).
[/list]

[heading]How it works[/heading]

[spoiler]
[b]The starting point.[/b] DBVO ships no SKSE plugin of its own: it's built entirely from Papyrus, ConsoleUtil, and an edited dialogue menu. Your voiced line is played through ConsoleUtil's [font="Courier New"]Player.SpeakSound "DBVO/<file>.fuz"[/font], which starts the sound and returns nothing: there's no "this line finished" callback to wait on. So the NPC's reply is gated by a plain timer inside [font="Courier New"]dialoguemenu.swf[/font], started when you pick a topic:

[quote][font="Courier New"]// DBVO's reply timing, in dialoguemenu.swf
words = topicText.split(" (")[0].split(" ").length;   // word count, minus "(Persuade)" etc.
delay = round(words * 200) + 1400;                    // ~300 wpm guess + a flat 1400 ms pad
setTimeout("topicClicked", delay);                    // NPC replies after delay[/font][/quote]

The reply lands at a time guessed from word count. It can't know your voice pack's actual speed, so it's wrong in both directions: a flat 1400 ms pad on top, and 200 ms/word that fast AI packs beat easily. The fix needs to know when the audio actually ends, which means reaching the sound itself, which means a DLL.

[b]The hook.[/b] This mod is a small SKSE plugin (CommonLibSSE-NG). At load it uses MinHook to detour the engine's speak-sound function, the same one ConsoleUtil's [font="Courier New"]Player.SpeakSound[/font] dispatches into. It's addressed only by Address Library ID (SE 36541 / AE 37542), which is why one DLL covers both SE and AE: the right address is resolved at runtime. The detour calls the original function first, so the engine builds and starts the sound handle normally (lip-sync intact), then inspects the call. Only if the speaker is the player and the path begins with "DBVO/" does it act: every other sound in the game passes straight through, untouched. For a matching line it does three things: scales the line's volume to the MCM slider, keeps a copy of the sound handle so it can be cut later, and arms the reply watcher.

[i](The handle copy is safe to keep: a BSSoundHandle is a tiny value keyed by a sound ID, and every operation re-resolves the live sound through the audio manager by that ID, so a copy made when the line started still controls it moments later. Each new line overwrites the slot, so only the current line is ever cuttable, which is exactly the right scope.)[/i]

[b]Reply on line-end.[/b] One background thread starts when the game loads and runs for the whole session. It sleeps ~30 ms per tick; idle it's a near-no-op, never pinning a core. While a line is armed it polls the retained handle: still playing, or stopped? The check runs off the main thread (the audio-manager calls are safe there), and a latch makes sure the thread has actually seen the line playing before it's allowed to detect an end, so the brief window between arming and the audio starting can't be misread as "already finished." The moment it sees playing → stopped, it claims the event exactly once and hands a single task to the main thread. That last hop matters: Scaleform (the Flash UI layer) isn't thread-safe, so the swf can only be touched on the main thread. The task calls one new swf function, [font="Courier New"]dbvoOnPlayerLineEnded[/font], which cancels the word-count timer and reschedules the reply after your configured gap. Net result: the reply is driven by the real end of the audio, plus a gap you choose, on every line.

[i](Why a dedicated thread and not the engine's task queue? An earlier version re-scheduled itself on the main thread's task queue each tick. That froze the game solid: SKSE drains that queue to empty every frame, so a task that re-queues itself spins the frame forever. Polling on its own thread with a real sleep is what fixed it.)[/i]

[b]The backstop.[/b] The swf still keeps a word-count timer, but deliberately a long one, and only as a fallback:

[quote][font="Courier New"]// swf backstop: only fires if the DLL never reports the end
delay = round(words * 300) + 2000;   // generous on purpose: must outlast any real line[/font][/quote]

When the DLL is present and working, [font="Courier New"]dbvoOnPlayerLineEnded[/font] clears this long before it expires, so you never see it. It exists so the mod degrades gracefully: without the DLL (or on a line whose end somehow isn't detected) dialogue still advances, just on the old (now very forgiving) guess instead of hanging forever.

[b]The gap.[/b] [font="Courier New"]dbvoOnPlayerLineEnded[/font] waits the MCM's "gap" value (0–1000 ms, default 250) before firing the reply, so you can dial the pacing from instant to a clear beat between lines.

[b]Player-voice volume.[/b] Below 100%, same hook, no extra machinery: it scales the line's handle to the slider. The value is pushed down from the MCM through a tiny Papyrus native and read in the hook; at 100% the hook is a pure pass-through, so leaving it default changes nothing. Above 100% (1.1.0) the engine cannot help — it clamps a sound's volume at 1.0 and caps its XAudio2 push at 0 dB — so a second hook sits on the engine's own volume-apply function ([font="Courier New"]BSXAudio2GameSound::SetVolumeImpl[/font], the one place the XAudio2 voice's gain is written): it lets the engine set its value, then multiplies the voice's gain for your line only, matched by sound id on the audio thread. Every later engine re-apply (a fade, a settings change) runs through the same hook, so the boost holds for the whole line. It touches only your own DBVO line; every other sound in the game keeps its own volume.

[b]Skip and interrupt: clean cuts.[/b] The swf raises a mod event in two cases and the DLL turns each into a clean stop:

[list]
[*][b]You skip your own line (E / left-click).[/b] The menu advances immediately and the DLL fades the retained player handle out over a few milliseconds rather than hard-stopping it: a hard stop mid-waveform clicks audibly; a short fade doesn't. It also stands the reply watcher down, so the fade's own playing → stopped transition can't fire a second reply. (A short debounce stops the same click that picked the topic from instantly skipping it.)
[*][b]You pick a new topic while the NPC is mid-reply.[/b] This one's fiddlier: the NPC's topic voice doesn't live where you'd expect, and the engine's "pause dialogue" only pauses, a multi-segment reply keeps going. So the DLL does both: it fades the currently-playing segment to silence and tells the engine to stop advancing to the next segment. Cutting the audio also leaves the NPC's mouth frozen open mid-word, so it snaps the face back to neutral, clearing the lip-sync keyframes under the same lock the engine's lip-sync writer uses, so the reset actually sticks instead of being overwritten next frame. (This is the same full-face-revert other mods like PhotoMode use.)
[/list]

[b]Tidying up.[/b] When the dialogue menu closes, the watcher is disarmed, so a half-finished line can't leak its reply into your next conversation. And firing a reply into a closed menu is a no-op anyway: the call simply finds no menu and does nothing.
[/spoiler]

[heading]Source[/heading]

Full source is on GitHub, 0BSD-licensed: [url=https://github.com/myaiexp/skyrim-headless-mods]myaiexp/skyrim-headless-mods[/url] (this mod lives under [font="Courier New"]mods/DBVODialogueTweaks[/font]).

It's all built headlessly on Linux: no Creation Kit, no SSEEdit, no GUI tooling. The DLL is cross-compiled against CommonLibSSE-NG, the swf is recompiled with ffdec, the Papyrus scripts compile from the command line, and the [font="Courier New"].esp[/font] is generated with Mutagen; a single build script produces every file the mod ships.

[heading]Permissions and credits[/heading]

Built on [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/84329]Dragonborn Voice Over[/url] by [b]MathiewMay[/b], with permission received from the author. The bundled [font="Courier New"]dialoguemenu.swf[/font] is DBVO's asset recompiled with these tweaks. All credit for DBVO goes to MathiewMay.

The four UI-overhaul menu styles are built on the DBVO-patched menus published for those mods on DBVO's own OLD FILES tab, and each one keeps that mod's dialogue-menu design — only the menu's script is changed. Every layout, font and colour in them is its author's work, used under that mod's permissions, and the credit for how those menus look belongs to them:
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/75188]Untarnished UI[/url] by [b]Vor[/b]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/60837]Dear Diary Dark Mode[/url] by [b]uranreactor[/b]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/49881]NORDIC UI[/url] by [b]outobugi[/b]
[/list]
If you are one of those authors and would rather this menu style were not shipped here, say so and I will pull it.
